From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Sep 1994 17:06:52 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-9-15] question for FAQ

dV/dt writes:
> I am opposed to putting any of my views into the FAQ on topics that I feel
> are appropriate for discussion on the list (those that don't have easy
> answers).  I have my own opinions on these questions, but I don't want to
> represent my views as "the extropian consensus" if there is significant
> disagreement among extropians on that topic.  If *you* feel there is an
> "easy answer" to some of these thorny questions, you're free to write up
> that answer for the FAQ; but anything that requires more than a page or so
> to sort out is probably an open issue and not FAQ-suitable (IMNSHO).

Oh gosh, I *don't* have easy answers! Perhaps FAQ would more accurately
mean  "frequently ANSWERED questions?" 

Some groups do put questions into their FAQ's that don't have easy answers.
If a  question comes up frequently and the discussion tends to fall into
two or three common ruts, a paragraph or two along the lines of:

"This comes up frequently. Two common opinions are 
  X, with points a,b,c as discussed in these references and
  Y, with points d,e,f as discussed in these references."

can help reduce redundancy and can be a good way to give ideas and
information to newcomers.

A simple example, although I can't fill in the blanks, might be:

"Q: Is there Extropian music?"  
"A: There's no such thing as Extropianly Correct music <smiley> but some
common areas of interest are:

  -Artists who are influenced in their work by Extropean ideas or resources
   (such as ________)
  -Music as an aid to learning,expanded or altered states of consciousness,
   relaxation, and other types of personal expansion (such as______)
  -Music that list members find stimulating (such as____)"

> I don't consider anyone "extropian" who isn't a (small-L) libertarian;
> furthermore, I apply this distinction recursively: I don't consider {anyone
> who thinks that a non-libertarian can be an extropian} to be an extropian;
> dot dot dot.  If you think it's OK for some criminal gang calling itself a
> government to push around others who are trying to mind their own business,
> then I think you're still The Enemy.  I will generate an additional FAQ
> answer to cover this point.

Gleep!  Please, if you do, consider that some of us are in "transitional
states" and possibly might be headed towards libertarian conclusions (or
not) but are still looking for new information and new ideas. Is everyone
whose mind is still open to new possibilities really your Enemy? 

As some other members have pointed out, the word "libertarian" is hard to
define, especially since so many people with diverse views are all claiming
to be libertarians (big and small l's.) I'd suggest that sticking with
Max More's statement of principles might be a good way to handle the 
libertarian question.

thanks, Betsy





